          "text": "mycorrhizal partners.\n\nIn a mycorrhizal\nassociation, the fungal mycelia use their extensive network of hyphae and large surface area in contact\nwith the soil to channel water and minerals from the soil into the plant. In exchange, the plant supplies\nthe products of photosynthesis to fuel the metabolism of the fungus. There are several basic types of mycorrhizae. Ectomycorrhizae (“outside” mycorrhizae) depend on\nfungi enveloping the roots in a sheath (called a mantle). Hyphae grow from the mantle into the root and\nenvelope the outer layers of the root cells in a network of hyphae called a Hartig net (Figure 24.21). The\nfungal partner can belong to the Ascomycota, Basidiomycota or Zygomycota.",
